cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  ProofLayer: Control Coverage Screening for Government
  Cybersecurity Policies via Multi-LLM Orchestration
message: >-
  If you use this software or data, please cite the published paper
  given under preferred-citation.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Trivedi
    given-names: Devharsh
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6374-7249'
    affiliation: 'City University of New York, NY, USA'
repository-code: 'https://github.com/devharsh/ProofLayer'
abstract: >-
  Control coverage screening engine and analysis code for a keyword-based
  mapping study of ten Maryland state and local government
  cybersecurity policy documents against 22 controls drawn from 13
  NIST SP 800-53 Rev 5 families. Reading each document in full, the
  study reports a mean coverage of 62.8 percent, and characterises
  what the metric tracks: coverage correlates with document length
  at r = 0.690, 18.1 percent of covered pairs carry a negation cue
  near the match, and 44.2 percent rest on a single keyword. An
  earlier version reported 49.1 percent, computed on the first
  fifteen pages of each document only; that figure is superseded.
  The repository also contains the ProofLayer system itself,
  including an LLM gap-analysis stage and a cost-aware model
  routing layer, both of which are described but not evaluated in
  the paper.
keywords:
  - cybersecurity policy analysis
  - control coverage screening
  - NIST SP 800-53
  - government cybersecurity
  - keyword matching
  - measurement validity
  - large language models
  - Maryland
license: MIT
